## Thread.Abort → .NET 8 Migration

### Problem

`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. Your code has three patterns that must change:

| Pattern Found | Location | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `ProcessLoop` | `CancellationToken` checked in loop |
| **ThreadAbortException catch + ResetAbort** | `ProcessLoop` catch block | `OperationCanceledException` catch |
| **Thread.Abort() call** | `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — same role as the old
                // ThreadAbortException catch block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort equivalent needed: we simply continue
                // to the loop condition, which will exit naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel(); // signals cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for the thread to finish
        if (_workerThread != null && _workerThread.IsAlive)
        {
            if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
            {
                // Thread didn't stop in time. Log a warning — you cannot
                // force-kill it in modern .NET. See pitfalls below.
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Key Changes Explained

1. **`Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — signals the token instead of injecting an exception.

2. **`catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)`** — the `when` guard prevents catching unrelated OCEs.

3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` removed** — no equivalent needed. The loop condition (`!token.IsCancellationRequested`) decides whether to continue or exit.

4. **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `_workerThread.Join(2s)`** — actually waits for the thread instead of hoping it finishes during a blind sleep.

5. **`GetNextItem()` must accept the token** — this is the most critical change. If it blocks (e.g., `BlockingCollection.Take`, `Socket.Receive`, `Monitor.Wait`), it will **never see cancellation** unless you pass the token:

```csharp
// Before: blocks indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could interrupt
var item = _queue.Take();

// After: throws OperationCanceledException when cancelled
var item = _queue.Take(token);
```

### Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `Thread.Sleep`, `queue.Take()`, `socket.Receive` ignore the token. Replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)`, `queue.Take(token)`, or async I/O. |
| **No fallback if thread won't stop** | Unlike `Abort()`, there's no force-kill. If `GetNextItem` calls uncooperative code, move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()`. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | It's `IDisposable` — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Always use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions. |
| **Forgetting to forward the token down the call chain** | Every method in the chain must accept and forward the token, or cancellation stalls at that point. |

### The Fundamental Shift

.NET Framework: **preemptive** — the runtime forcibly injects `ThreadAbortException` at any point, even mid-expression.

.NET 8: **cooperative** — your code must voluntarily check for cancellation. If it never checks, it never stops. Design checkpoint frequency based on acceptable shutdown latency.
